<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chao Phraya River</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Chao Phraya is the main river of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thailand">Thailand</a>, formed at Nakhon Sawan by the meeting of the Ping and the Nan and flowing south past <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayutthaya-kingdom">Ayutthaya</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bangkok">Bangkok</a> into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-thailand">Gulf of Thailand</a>. Its fertile flood plain supported the Dvaravati towns, Lavo and Suphannaphum, and the successive Thai capitals of Ayutthaya, Thonburi and Bangkok, whose wealth rested on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> and on trade carried along the river.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chao-phraya-river.jpg" alt="Chao Phraya River" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: This Photo was taken by Supanut Arunoprayote. … · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chao_Phraya_River_-_Bangkok_-_northwards_-_from_King_Taksin_Bridge_-_2021.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Chao Phraya begins at the confluence of the Ping and Nan rivers at Nakhon Sawan and flows south for 372 kilometres to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bangkok">Bangkok</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-thailand">Gulf of Thailand</a>. At Chai Nat it splits off the Tha Chin River, which runs parallel and reaches the gulf at Samut Sakhon. Its watershed, 157,924 square kilometres, is the largest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thailand">Thailand</a>.</p><p>The plain falls only about 80 metres over 500 kilometres from the hills to the sea, so the rivers run sluggishly and boats could be poled or paddled upstream for most of the year, which made water the most convenient transport before the modern era. The delta took its present form late: the coast reached the site of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bangkok">Bangkok</a> between 3000 and 1000 BCE and settled near its present position by 500 CE.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The basin saw the rise of some of the earliest civilisations of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a>, notably Dvaravati from the 7th to the 11th century. The river was crucial to the Lavo kingdom and to its successors, and formed the basis of the Ayodhya Kingdom, incorporated into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayutthaya-kingdom">Ayutthaya Kingdom</a> in the 14th century. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">Rice</a> growing intensified from the 13th century under Sukhothai and Ayutthaya with the introduction of floating rice from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a>.</p><p>During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayutthaya-kingdom">Ayutthaya</a> period shortcut canals were dug across river loops, the first in 1538 on the order of King Chairachathirat, and the river's course has since followed many of them.</p><p>Rattanakosin, now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bangkok">Bangkok</a>, was founded in 1782 on the east bank, a position that protected it from Burmese invasions from the west. Canalisation and cultivation of the southern swamps followed, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thailand">Thailand</a> began to export <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> in 1855. The name Chao Phraya is documented in writing only from the reign of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongkut">Mongkut</a> in the 19th century; earlier maps usually called the river the Menam. It probably comes from Bang Chao Phraya, the settlement now called Samut Prakan, recorded from about 1660.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The provinces along the river, from Nakhon Sawan to Samut Prakan, include <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayutthaya-kingdom">Ayutthaya</a>, Nonthaburi and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bangkok">Bangkok</a>, among the most historically significant and densely populated in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thailand">Thailand</a>. Its many canals, called khlong, irrigate the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> paddies of the plain, and in Bangkok the river is a major transport artery for ferries and long-tail boats. The original swamp forests of the lowlands have almost entirely been cleared for farming and cities.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Chao Phraya River” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…I. He built his capital on an island formed where the Lopburi and Pa Sak rivers meet the <b>Chao Phraya</b>, a site that could be defended by water in the flood season, commanded the rice lands of the central plain and lay within reach of seagoing ships from…</blockquote><a href="/empire/ayutthaya-kingdom">Ayutthaya Kingdom · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Taksin, a provincial governor of partly Chinese descent, rallied resistance, expelled the occupying garrisons and ruled from Thonburi on the west bank of the <b>Chao Phraya River</b>, but by 1782 his rule had become erratic and a revolt broke out in the capital.</blockquote><a href="/empire/rattanakosin-kingdom">Rattanakosin Kingdom · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Sukhothai lies on the Yom River, a tributary of the <b>Chao Phraya</b>, in the plain of north-central Thailand.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sukhothai-kingdom">Sukhothai Kingdom · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Having moved his capital that year to an island in the <b>Chao Phraya River</b>, where he founded Ayutthaya, Ramathibodi I was formally established as ruler of a centralised state.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/march-4">4 March in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Moated towns appear in the <b>Chao Phraya</b> valley from around the 6th and 7th centuries, built by Mon-speaking communities.</blockquote><a href="/state/dvaravati">Dvaravati</a></li><li><blockquote>As Khmer power waned in the 13th century, Lavo was linked by marriage and politics with Suphannaphum, and in 1351 U Thong founded the Ayutthaya Kingdom on the <b>Chao Phraya</b>, combining both.</blockquote><a href="/state/lavo-kingdom">Lavo Kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
